🧑‍💻 SSH (Secure Shell) – Interview Cheat Sheet
🔑 1. What is SSH?
SSH (Secure Shell) is a protocol used to securely access and manage remote servers over a network.
Key idea:
Secure remote login
Encrypted communication
Replaces insecure tools like Telnet
🔐 2. SSH Authentication Types
1. Password-based
Login using username + password (less secure)
2. Key-based (Recommended)
Uses cryptographic key pair instead of password
🔑 3. SSH Key Pair Concept
Private Key → kept on client machine (secret)
Public Key → stored on server
How it works:
Server stores public key
Client proves identity using private key
Server verifies match → grants access
📁 4. Important File (Server Side)
~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Explanation:
Stores all allowed public keys
Each line = one authorized device/user
🔐 5. SSH Directory Permissions
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
Only owner can access .ssh directory
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Only owner can read/write authorized_keys file
➕ 6. Add SSH Public Key
Method 1 (recommended)
echo "ssh-ed25519 AAAA... user@device" >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Method 2 (manual input)
cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Then:

Paste key → CTRL + D to save
👀 7. View Stored Keys
cat ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Displays all authorized public keys
🔢 8. View Keys with Line Numbers
nl ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Helps identify each key separately
🕒 9. View Recently Added Keys
tail ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Shows latest added SSH keys
🔄 10. SSH Login Flow (VERY IMPORTANT)
1. Client connects to server
2. Server checks authorized_keys
3. Server sends challenge
4. Client signs challenge using private key
5. Server verifies signature
6. Access granted if valid
⚠️ 11. Common Mistakes
- Wrong file permissions → SSH fails
- Public key not added correctly
- Multiple keys in one line (invalid)
- Using private key on server (wrong)
🔐 12. Why SSH is Secure
- Uses encryption (RSA / Ed25519)
- Private key never leaves client machine
- Prevents password-based attacks
🧪 13. Useful Linux Commands
whoami   # current user
id       # user + groups
sudo -l  # check sudo permissions
💡 14. Real-World Usage
- Remote server management (AWS, Azure, GCP)
- Git authentication (GitHub, GitLab)
- DevOps deployments
- Kubernetes / Docker access
🧠 15. One-Line Interview Answer
SSH is a secure protocol that uses public-private key cryptography to authenticate users and enable encrypted remote server access without passwords.
